camera-sign for Debian
======================

The package installs the command and a systemd user unit, but enables the
unit for nobody. To use it:

1. Set up the plug in the Kasa app, and give it a fixed address with a DHCP
   reservation. "kasa discover" (from python3-kasa) finds it.

2. Tell camera-sign where the plug is:

     mkdir -p ~/.config/camera-sign
     cp /usr/share/doc/camera-sign/examples/env.example ~/.config/camera-sign/env
     editor ~/.config/camera-sign/env

   CAMERA_SIGN_HOST is the plug's address. Newer Kasa firmware also needs
   CAMERA_SIGN_USERNAME and CAMERA_SIGN_PASSWORD, the TP-Link account the
   plug was set up with; "camera-sign on" fails with an authentication error
   without them.

3. Check it, then start the service:

     set -a; . ~/.config/camera-sign/env; set +a
     camera-sign on && sleep 3 && camera-sign off
     systemctl --user enable --now camera-sign
     journalctl --user -u camera-sign -f

The sign inserts to print are in /usr/share/camera-sign/print/: on-air.svg
prints landscape, camera-on.svg portrait. Print at 100% on the rough side of
inkjet transparency film.

